      "updated": 1781510890685,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Scaling to 10K Clicks per Second\n- Horizontal scaling at every layer: The click processor service scales horizontally behind a load balancer.\nStreams like Kafka/Kinesis scale via sharding, and Flink scales by running parallel jobs — one per shard —\nhandling aggregation independently.  \n- Smart sharding strategy: Shard the stream by AdId so all events for the same ad land in the same shard,\nenabling parallel and independent processing. For self-managed OLAP databases like ClickHouse, shard by\nAdvertiserId so all of an advertiser's ads live on one node — speeding up multi-ad dashboard queries.\nManaged warehouses (Snowflake, BigQuery) handle scaling automatically.\n- Hot shard problem: A viral ad (e.g., Nike + LeBron) sends massive traffic to a single shard, causing latency\nspikes and potential data loss. All clicks for that AdId hit the same partition, overwhelming it.\n- Hot shard fix - partition key suffixing: Append a random suffix to popular AdIds (identified by ad spend or\nhistorical volume), turning the key into AdId:0-N. This spreads load across multiple partitions. When Flink\nwrites to OLAP, it strips the suffix and uses the original AdId — since writes are upserts with SUM\naggregation, concurrent writes from different partitions combine correctly without data loss.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n2) Ensuring No Click Data Loss\n- Stream-level durability built-in: Kafka and Kinesis are distributed, fault-tolerant, and highly available by\ndefault — Kafka replicates across brokers, Kinesis across availability zones. Enabling a retention period (e.g.,\n7 days) means even if the stream processor crashes, it can come back up and replay missed events directly\nfrom the stream.\n- Checkpointing — useful but often overkill here: Flink supports checkpointing (periodically saving state to\nS3) so it can resume after failure. This shines for large aggregation windows (days/weeks), but with our tiny\nminute-level windows, we'd lose at most a minute of work — easily recoverable by replaying the stream.\nPushing back on unnecessary checkpointing is a strong signal of senior-level judgment in interviews.\n- Periodic reconciliation for correctness: Low latency and perfect correctness are often at odds. To catch\ntransient errors, bad deploys, or out-of-order events, continuously archive raw clicks to a data lake (S3)\nusing Kafka Connect S3 Sink or Kinesis Data Firehose — no extra load on Flink. Then run a daily Spark batch\njob to re-aggregate from raw events and compare against the stream processor's output, fixing any\ndiscrepancies in the OLAP DB.\n- Lambda Architecture - best of both worlds: This pattern combines a speed layer (Flink) for low-latency\nresults with a batch layer (Spark) acting as the source of truth for correctness. The batch layer periodically\nreconciles and corrects any inaccuracies from the speed layer, giving you fast and accurate click data.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n3) Preventing Click Abuse & Enforcing Idempotency\n- Naive approach (userId + adId dedup) is insufficient: Requiring login and deduping on userId + adId works\nonly if all users are authenticated and — more critically — breaks retargeting, where the same user is\nintentionally shown the same ad multiple times. We actually want one click per user per ad instance\n(impression), not per ad overall.\n- Impression ID as idempotency key: The Ad Placement Service generates a unique impression ID every time\nan ad is rendered (1000 views = 1000 unique IDs). This ID is sent to the browser with the ad and returned\nwith the click event, allowing the system to dedup on impression ID — which correctly handles retargeting.\n- Dedup must happen before the stream, not in Flink: Since Flink aggregates in windows, a duplicate click\nstraddling a minute boundary would be double-counted. Instead, the Click Processor checks a Redis cache for\nthe impression ID — if present, drop it; if not, write to the stream first, then update the cache (writing\nfirst ensures no click loss on cache failure; rare duplicates get cleaned up by reconciliation).\n- HMAC signing prevents forged impression IDs: Without protection, attackers could spam fake unique\nimpression IDs and inflate clicks. Sign the impression ID + adId with an HMAC secret key before sending to\nthe browser, then verify on click. HMAC is microsecond-fast (no asymmetric crypto) and also prevents\nreplaying valid IDs against different ads.\n- Cache scaling is a non-issue: Even at 100M impressions/day x 16 bytes = 1.6 GB = tiny. Use Redis Cluster\nfor horizontal scale, with replicas + persistence (RDB/AOF) for failover, and the cache will comfortably\nhandle the load.\n\n4) Ensuring Low-Latency Advertiser Queries\n- Already largely solved by pre-aggregation in the pipeline: Whether using batch (Spark) or stream processing\n(Flink), data is pre-aggregated before landing in the OLAP database. Advertisers query already-summarized\ndata instead of scanning raw click events, making typical queries fast out of the box.\n- Large time-window queries are the remaining bottleneck: Aggregating over days, weeks, months, or years at\nquery time can still be slow because the OLAP DB has to scan and roll up many minute-level rows. This is\nwhere additional optimization is needed.\n- Pre-aggregate at multiple granularities via rollup tables: Run a nightly cron job that aggregates minute-level\ndata into daily, weekly, or monthly rollup tables. Advertisers query the coarser table for high-level views (e.g.,\nyearly trends) and drill down into finer tables only when needed — trading storage space for dramatic query\nspeedups on the most common access patterns (a form of caching).",
      "fontSize": 19.47692467305834,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Scaling to 10K Clicks per Second\n- Horizontal scaling at every layer: The click processor service scales horizontally behind a load balancer. Streams like Kafka/Kinesis scale via sharding, and Flink scales by running parallel jobs — one per shard — handling aggregation independently.  \n- Smart sharding strategy: Shard the stream by AdId so all events for the same ad land in the same shard, enabling parallel and independent processing. For self-managed OLAP databases like ClickHouse, shard by AdvertiserId so all of an advertiser's ads live on one node — speeding up multi-ad dashboard queries. Managed warehouses (Snowflake, BigQuery) handle scaling automatically.\n- Hot shard problem: A viral ad (e.g., Nike + LeBron) sends massive traffic to a single shard, causing latency spikes and potential data loss. All clicks for that AdId hit the same partition, overwhelming it.\n- Hot shard fix - partition key suffixing: Append a random suffix to popular AdIds (identified by ad spend or historical volume), turning the key into AdId:0-N. This spreads load across multiple partitions. When Flink writes to OLAP, it strips the suffix and uses the original AdId — since writes are upserts with SUM aggregation, concurrent writes from different partitions combine correctly without data loss.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n2) Ensuring No Click Data Loss\n- Stream-level durability built-in: Kafka and Kinesis are distributed, fault-tolerant, and highly available by default — Kafka replicates across brokers, Kinesis across availability zones. Enabling a retention period (e.g., 7 days) means even if the stream processor crashes, it can come back up and replay missed events directly from the stream.\n- Checkpointing — useful but often overkill here: Flink supports checkpointing (periodically saving state to S3) so it can resume after failure. This shines for large aggregation windows (days/weeks), but with our tiny minute-level windows, we'd lose at most a minute of work — easily recoverable by replaying the stream. Pushing back on unnecessary checkpointing is a strong signal of senior-level judgment in interviews.\n- Periodic reconciliation for correctness: Low latency and perfect correctness are often at odds. To catch transient errors, bad deploys, or out-of-order events, continuously archive raw clicks to a data lake (S3) using Kafka Connect S3 Sink or Kinesis Data Firehose — no extra load on Flink. Then run a daily Spark batch job to re-aggregate from raw events and compare against the stream processor's output, fixing any discrepancies in the OLAP DB.\n- Lambda Architecture - best of both worlds: This pattern combines a speed layer (Flink) for low-latency results with a batch layer (Spark) acting as the source of truth for correctness. The batch layer periodically reconciles and corrects any inaccuracies from the speed layer, giving you fast and accurate click data.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n3) Preventing Click Abuse & Enforcing Idempotency\n- Naive approach (userId + adId dedup) is insufficient: Requiring login and deduping on userId + adId works only if all users are authenticated and — more critically — breaks retargeting, where the same user is intentionally shown the same ad multiple times. We actually want one click per user per ad instance (impression), not per ad overall.\n- Impression ID as idempotency key: The Ad Placement Service generates a unique impression ID every time an ad is rendered (1000 views = 1000 unique IDs). This ID is sent to the browser with the ad and returned with the click event, allowing the system to dedup on impression ID — which correctly handles retargeting.\n- Dedup must happen before the stream, not in Flink: Since Flink aggregates in windows, a duplicate click straddling a minute boundary would be double-counted. Instead, the Click Processor checks a Redis cache for the impression ID — if present, drop it; if not, write to the stream first, then update the cache (writing first ensures no click loss on cache failure; rare duplicates get cleaned up by reconciliation).\n- HMAC signing prevents forged impression IDs: Without protection, attackers could spam fake unique impression IDs and inflate clicks. Sign the impression ID + adId with an HMAC secret key before sending to the browser, then verify on click. HMAC is microsecond-fast (no asymmetric crypto) and also prevents replaying valid IDs against different ads.\n- Cache scaling is a non-issue: Even at 100M impressions/day x 16 bytes = 1.6 GB = tiny. Use Redis Cluster for horizontal scale, with replicas + persistence (RDB/AOF) for failover, and the cache will comfortably handle the load.\n\n4) Ensuring Low-Latency Advertiser Queries\n- Already largely solved by pre-aggregation in the pipeline: Whether using batch (Spark) or stream processing (Flink), data is pre-aggregated before landing in the OLAP database. Advertisers query already-summarized data instead of scanning raw click events, making typical queries fast out of the box.\n- Large time-window queries are the remaining bottleneck: Aggregating over days, weeks, months, or years at query time can still be slow because the OLAP DB has to scan and roll up many minute-level rows. This is where additional optimization is needed.\n- Pre-aggregate at multiple granularities via rollup tables: Run a nightly cron job that aggregates minute-level data into daily, weekly, or monthly rollup tables. Advertisers query the coarser table for high-level views (e.g., yearly trends) and drill down into finer tables only when needed — trading storage space for dramatic query speedups on the most common access patterns (a form of caching).",
