    {
      "id": "nRZ97kOuSGG6Z7kpzO9iK",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 273.54731481162344,
      "y": 164.356989362901,
      "width": 1883.795654296875,
      "height": 50,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eebefa",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bQ1",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1310644061,
      "version": 492,
      "versionNonce": 1849320051,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781441717306,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "An Ad Click Aggregator is a system that collects and aggregates data on ad clicks. It is used by advertisers to track the performance of their ads and optimize their campaigns. For our\npurposes, we will assume these are ads displayed on a website or app, like Facebook.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "An Ad Click Aggregator is a system that collects and aggregates data on ad clicks. It is used by advertisers to track the performance of their ads and optimize their campaigns. For our purposes, we will assume these are ads displayed on a website or app, like Facebook.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "3l3B22jVKTzOzRxzUcwts",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1369.112630322708,
      "y": 271.2411714017653,
      "width": 336.19598388671875,
      "height": 35,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bQ2",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 899908701,
      "version": 3263,
      "versionNonce": 435431858,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781510908834,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Key Concepts on the go ",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Key Concepts on the go ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "aoui3fXAGxZFf4vIU88Wf",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1366.5684692860816,
      "y": 325.94236620432275,
      "width": 1041.9351226835824,
      "height": 2425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eebefa",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bQ4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1744401587,
      "version": 922,
      "versionNonce": 216540018,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781510908834,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) When a user clicks on an ad which was placed by the Ad Placement Service, we will send a request to\nour /click endpoint, which will track the click and then redirect the user to the advertiser's website. Add\nplacement service is like a black box for now.\n\n2) Handling The redirect :\n- Client Side:\n    > When a user clicks on the ad, the browser will automatically redirect them to the target URL. It's\nsimple, straightforward, and requires no additional server side logic. We would then, in parallel, POST to\nour /click endpoint to track the click.\n    > downside with this approach is that users could go to an advertiser's website without us knowing\nabout it. This could lead to discrepancies in our click data\n- Servier Side:\n    > user click on the ad, which will then send a request to our server. Our server can then track the click\nand respond with a redirect to the advertiser's website via a 302 (redirect) status code. \n    > challenge is added load and complexity\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n3) Querying Ad Click metrics\n- Bad Solution:\n    > store and qry from same database.\n    > store in db based on userid and ad id. then query based on the ad id using a group by.\n    > this solution sucks. The database will quickly become a bottleneck as we scale up to 10k clicks per\nsecond. The GROUP BY query will be slow and inefficient, and we won't be able to provide low latency\nqueries to advertisers\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n- Good Solution:\n    > use a separate analytics database with pre-aggregated data for low-latency queries.\n    > Raw click -> Event DB -> Batch processing -> Analytics DB.\n    > Event Db: Optimized for very high write throughput. Uses LSM tree storage structure. Writes first\ngo to an append-only commit log (durability). Then to an in-memory sorted memtable. Memtables are\nperiodically flushed to disk as SSTables. SSTables are good for row reads but poor for range queries and\naggregations. This is why batch pre-aggregation is needed.\n    > Batch Processing(Apache Spark): Distributed computing engine using the MapReduce paradigm. Reads\nraw events in parallel chunks. Aggregates events by ad ID and minute timestamp. Writes aggregated\nresults into the analytics DB.\n    > BOE Calculations: 10K clicks/sec with 5-minute batches = 3M events per batch. ~100 bytes per\nevent = ~300 MB per batch. Small enough for a single machine, but Spark gives scalability. Spark also\noffers a clean MapReduce model that's easy to explain in interviews.\n    > Analytical Db: Good choices: Redshift, Snowflake, BigQuery. Uses columnar storage for fast\naggregation queries. Excellent for COUNT, SUM, AVG over millions of rows. Optimized for read-heavy\nanalytical workloads.\n    > challenge: Batch processing causes stale data and cascades under load: Advertisers always see data\nthat is a few minutes old, and during traffic spikes the event store faces write pressure while batch jobs\ntake longer than their interval — leading to increasingly delayed insights exactly when real-time visibility\nmatters most (e.g., big ad launches).  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n- Great Solution:\n    > Stream-based architecture replaces batch jobs: Click events flow directly into a stream (Kafka or\nKinesis), where a stream processor (Flink or Spark Streaming) aggregates them in real-time and writes\nresults to the OLAP database for near real-time advertiser queries.\n    > Why Flink over plain Kafka consumers: Flink provides windowed aggregations with event-time\nsemantics (handles out-of-order events correctly), watermarks (knows when to safely close a window),\nexactly-once processing guarantees, and built-in fault tolerance with state recovery — all of which are\npainful and error-prone to build manually.\n    > Event time vs processing time: Flink uses event time (when the click actually happened) instead of\nprocessing time (when Flink received it), ensuring accurate minute-bucket aggregations even when events\narrive late or out of order.\n    > Much finer latency control than batch: Stream aggregation windows can be shrunk to a few seconds,\nwhich is impractical for Spark batch jobs due to overhead. Even better, Flink can aggregate on minute\nboundaries but flush partial results every few seconds — giving low latency while still producing clean,\ncomplete minute-level aggregates.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) When a user clicks on an ad which was placed by the Ad Placement Service, we will send a request to our /click endpoint, which will track the click and then redirect the user to the advertiser's website. Add placement service is like a black box for now.\n\n2) Handling The redirect :\n- Client Side:\n    > When a user clicks on the ad, the browser will automatically redirect them to the target URL. It's simple, straightforward, and requires no additional server side logic. We would then, in parallel, POST to our /click endpoint to track the click.\n    > downside with this approach is that users could go to an advertiser's website without us knowing about it. This could lead to discrepancies in our click data\n- Servier Side:\n    > user click on the ad, which will then send a request to our server. Our server can then track the click and respond with a redirect to the advertiser's website via a 302 (redirect) status code. \n    > challenge is added load and complexity\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n3) Querying Ad Click metrics\n- Bad Solution:\n    > store and qry from same database.\n    > store in db based on userid and ad id. then query based on the ad id using a group by.\n    > this solution sucks. The database will quickly become a bottleneck as we scale up to 10k clicks per second. The GROUP BY query will be slow and inefficient, and we won't be able to provide low latency queries to advertisers\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n- Good Solution:\n    > use a separate analytics database with pre-aggregated data for low-latency queries.\n    > Raw click -> Event DB -> Batch processing -> Analytics DB.\n    > Event Db: Optimized for very high write throughput. Uses LSM tree storage structure. Writes first go to an append-only commit log (durability). Then to an in-memory sorted memtable. Memtables are periodically flushed to disk as SSTables. SSTables are good for row reads but poor for range queries and aggregations. This is why batch pre-aggregation is needed.\n    > Batch Processing(Apache Spark): Distributed computing engine using the MapReduce paradigm. Reads raw events in parallel chunks. Aggregates events by ad ID and minute timestamp. Writes aggregated results into the analytics DB.\n    > BOE Calculations: 10K clicks/sec with 5-minute batches = 3M events per batch. ~100 bytes per event = ~300 MB per batch. Small enough for a single machine, but Spark gives scalability. Spark also offers a clean MapReduce model that's easy to explain in interviews.\n    > Analytical Db: Good choices: Redshift, Snowflake, BigQuery. Uses columnar storage for fast aggregation queries. Excellent for COUNT, SUM, AVG over millions of rows. Optimized for read-heavy analytical workloads.\n    > challenge: Batch processing causes stale data and cascades under load: Advertisers always see data that is a few minutes old, and during traffic spikes the event store faces write pressure while batch jobs take longer than their interval — leading to increasingly delayed insights exactly when real-time visibility matters most (e.g., big ad launches).  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n- Great Solution:\n    > Stream-based architecture replaces batch jobs: Click events flow directly into a stream (Kafka or Kinesis), where a stream processor (Flink or Spark Streaming) aggregates them in real-time and writes results to the OLAP database for near real-time advertiser queries.\n    > Why Flink over plain Kafka consumers: Flink provides windowed aggregations with event-time semantics (handles out-of-order events correctly), watermarks (knows when to safely close a window), exactly-once processing guarantees, and built-in fault tolerance with state recovery — all of which are painful and error-prone to build manually.\n    > Event time vs processing time: Flink uses event time (when the click actually happened) instead of processing time (when Flink received it), ensuring accurate minute-bucket aggregations even when events arrive late or out of order.\n    > Much finer latency control than batch: Stream aggregation windows can be shrunk to a few seconds, which is impractical for Spark batch jobs due to overhead. Even better, Flink can aggregate on minute boundaries but flush partial results every few seconds — giving low latency while still producing clean, complete minute-level aggregates.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
